# Local AppArmor override for the `unprivileged_userns` profile.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Ubuntu 24.04 (the GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` runner image) enables
# restricted unprivileged user namespaces by default. A process that
# creates a user namespace transitions into AppArmor's
# `unprivileged_userns` profile, which grants `userns`, `network`,
# `file rwlkm`, `signal`, `dbus`, `unix`, `mqueue` and `ptrace` -- but
# NOT `mount` and NOT `x`.
#
# The sandbox launcher's containment sequence needs BOTH:
#
#   * `mount` -- to make mount propagation private, remount the root
#     filesystem read-only, and bind-mount the artifact path writable;
#   * `x` -- to exec the parser after the namespace is established.
#
# Ubuntu's own profile ends with:
#
#   include if exists <local/unprivileged_userns>
#
# which is exactly the site-specific override mechanism this file uses.
# Adding rules there is the documented way to adjust the profile for a
# site, and it keeps the diff against the stock profile to two rules.
#
# SCOPE
#
# This is applied ONLY to the GitHub-hosted CI runner, in the
# dedicated `linux-network-isolation` job. It is not applied to the
# production deployment, whose host AppArmor policy is the operator's
# to configure.
#
# It is NOT `flags=(unconfined)`, it does not disable AppArmor, and it
# grants no capability. The container still runs as UID 1000 with no
# host CAP_SYS_ADMIN, and the shipped seccomp profile still governs
# which syscalls are permitted at all.
#
# The delta is asserted by scripts/dev/verify_apparmor_delta.py.

  mount,
  allow file x /{,**},
